          <p>
            The ProForm sheet also lists ProShox cushioning, a built-in fan, Bluetooth and the iFIT features
            SmartAdjust and ActivePulse. Treadmill Review Guru notes a USB-C port. ProForm footnotes its CHP
            ratings in the same way as its sister brand NordicTrack: a laboratory dynamometer figure for
            consumer comparison that does not denote operational horsepower in household use. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains what that means.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Deck, Motor and the 350 lb Rating</h2>
<p>
            The 20 by 60-inch belt is the standard full-size home deck. Sixty inches is the length running
            guidance generally settles on for adults who run rather than jog, and twenty inches is the common
            width. T3 notes the deck is narrower than some rivals', which is fair: NordicTrack's Commercial
            series and some Bowflex models run 22 inches wide. For most users of average build, 20 inches is
            fine; for broad-shouldered runners or anyone whose stride drifts, the extra two inches help. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            covers which sizes suit which heights and paces.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A firm deck</h3>
          <p>
            Treadmill Review Guru describes the Carbon Pro 9000's ProShox deck as firmer than earlier editions,
            which it suggests better matches running on a road. Firmness is a matter of preference rather than a
            fault: some runners like the feedback, and some walkers prefer a softer landing. If cushioning matters
            to you, stand on one before buying. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioning explainer</a>
            covers what cushioning does and does not do for joints.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The motor and its warranty</h3>
          <p>
            ProForm's 3.6 CHP rating is the highest it gives any current treadmill, and the same figure NordicTrack
            gives its T Series 16 and Commercial 1250. Treadmill Review Guru describes it as adequate for walking
            and running. The more telling number is the warranty: two years on the motor. That is the same term as
            the cheaper Carbon Pro 2000, and short against the lifetime motor cover on NordicTrack's Commercial
            1750 or the long motor terms on Bowflex and Sole machines. A two-year motor term on a flagship is a
            reason to look after the machine well and to price an extended plan honestly. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read each line.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 350 lb rating</h3>
          <p>
            A 350 lb maximum user weight is 50 lb more than the older Pro 9000 and 25 lb more than NordicTrack's
            T Series. It makes the Carbon Pro 9000 one of the higher-rated folding treadmills at its price. A rating
            is a ceiling, not a target, and a machine used near its limit every day works harder than one with
            margin. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-happens-if-you-exceed-treadmill-weight-limit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight limits</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-speed-screen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Incline, Speed and the 16-Inch Screen</h2>
<p>
            Incline runs from 0 to 12%, which covers incline walking, the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            at full setting and most hill intervals. There is no decline. Treadmill Review Guru timed a full
            incline change at about 46 seconds and calls it slow, which matters most in short hill intervals where
            the incline spends a good part of each rep getting to where it should be. If you plan workouts around
            quick incline changes, pick sessions with longer intervals or a machine with a faster incline motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            The 0 to 12 mph range is a five-minute mile at the top, which is beyond what most home runners need.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval workouts guide</a>
            covers sessions that fit the range.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The screen and iFIT</h3>
          <p>
            The 16-inch HD touchscreen is the reason to choose the 9000 over the Carbon Pro 2000, which has a
            10-inch screen. Treadmill Review Guru praises it and also notes more glare than it would like, so
            consider where windows and lights sit relative to the machine. Full iFIT content — trainer-led classes,
            scenic routes, and automatic speed and incline changes — needs a paid membership; without one the
            treadmill runs in manual mode. We do not quote the membership price because it changes. Independent
            reviews agree that much of the 9000's value is tied to the subscription. Our assessment of
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a>
            sets out the arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-or-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Walking or Running: How the Specification Reads</h2>
<p>
            For walkers, the Carbon Pro 9000 is generously specified. The 12% incline covers almost every
            incline-walking plan, the 350 lb rating leaves most users plenty of margin, the 7.75-inch step-up is
            easy to manage, and the 16-inch screen makes iFIT's walking and hiking routes easy to follow. The firm
            deck reviewers describe is the one point a walker who wants a soft landing should check in person. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking treadmills</a>
            guide covers what matters most for that use.
          </p>
          <p>
            For runners, it is adequate rather than ideal. The 60-inch belt and 12 mph cover most home running, and
            reviewers describe it as stable. The limits are the 20-inch width, the lack of decline for downhill
            practice, the slow incline changes for short hill repeats, and the two-year motor term for anyone
            logging high weekly mileage. A runner doing three or four easy sessions a week will be well served; a
            runner training hard most days should weigh a machine with a wider belt and longer motor cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Warranty, Delivery and Returns</h2>
<p>
            ProForm's manufacturer warranty on the Carbon Pro 9000 is ten years on the frame, two years on the
            motor and other parts, one year on the touchscreen and one year of labour. Treadmill Review Guru gives
            the same terms. Paid extensions are offered at checkout and by some retailers; price them against what
            a technician's call-out costs where you live. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair guide</a>
            gives typical costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Delivery, assembly and return terms depend on the seller. ProForm's own site and retailers such as
            Nebraska Furniture Mart set their own terms, and we could not read ProForm's trial terms on the day we
            checked. Its sister brand NordicTrack deducted a $249.99 return charge from in-home trial refunds, so
            read ProForm's current trial and return terms at checkout rather than assuming a return is free. The
            boxed weight is 249 lb, so plan the delivery route and expect assembly to need two people. Our
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembly guide</a>
            covers the job.
          </p>
          <p>
            Safety record: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall listings on 14 October
            2026 and found no recall of the Carbon Pro 9000, the older Pro 9000 or any current ProForm treadmill.
            iFIT's most recent ProForm recall, in July 2024, covered adjustable dumbbells.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Footprint, Folding and Ceiling Height</h2>
<p>
            The Carbon Pro 9000 measures 73.2 inches long, 34.6 wide and 60.7 tall on ProForm's sheet, repeated by
            Nebraska Furniture Mart. That is shorter than NordicTrack's Commercial series at 77.3 inches. It folds on
            the SpaceSaver frame with EasyLift assist. Garage Gym Reviews and T3 both describe it as large or heavy to
            move, which matches the 249 lb boxed weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            ProForm lists a 7.75-inch step-up height for its current range, the lowest of the machines we cover here,
            which helps ceiling clearance. A working rule is your height, plus the step-up, plus several inches for
            the bounce of a stride and the deck rising at full incline; the manual's guidance for your unit takes
            precedence. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space guide</a>
            gives typical clearances, and
            <a href="/where-to-put-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to put a treadmill</a>
            covers floors and noise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-reviewers-report" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Independent Reviewers Consistently Report</h2>
<p>
            We include a point only when more than one independent source makes it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Much of the value depends on iFIT.</strong> Treadmill Review Guru and T3 both treat the subscription as central to the machine and its main ongoing cost.</li>
            <li><strong>It is big and heavy to move.</strong> T3 says it is heavy to manoeuvre and Garage Gym Reviews notes a large footprint.</li>
            <li><strong>The build and motor are well regarded.</strong> T3 says it feels closer to a full-size machine than most folding rivals, and Treadmill Review Guru calls the motor adequate for walking and running.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Single-source points: Treadmill Review Guru reports slow incline changes, some screen glare and a firmer
            deck than earlier versions; T3 notes a narrower deck than some rivals. Each is worth checking in a
            showroom if it matters to you. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a>
            explains how to weigh them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Carbon Pro 9000 vs the Carbon Pro 2000 and NordicTrack</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Against the Carbon Pro 2000</h3>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-proform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm roundup</a>
            gives the Carbon Pro 2000 the same 20 by 60-inch belt, 0 to 12% incline, 12 mph and 350 lb rating, with
            a 10-inch screen; its motor rating is quoted inconsistently on ProForm's own page. On 13 October 2026 the
            two were about $300 apart on ProForm's site. That difference buys six more inches of screen and the
            confirmed 3.6 CHP rating. If you will follow iFIT classes in most sessions, the 9000 earns it; if you
            mostly walk or run to your own content, the Pro 2000 is the better value, and it is the roundup's
            overall pick for that reason.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Against the NordicTrack T Series 16</h3>
          <p>
            ProForm and NordicTrack are sister brands under iFIT, and the T Series 16 is the Carbon Pro 9000's
            nearest NordicTrack equivalent: a 16-inch tilting screen, a 3.6 CHP rating, a 20 by 60-inch belt, 0 to
            12% incline and 12 mph, with a 325 lb rating and an 8.3-inch step-up. It adds switchable SelectFlex
            cushioning; the ProForm adds 25 lb of rating and a lower step-up. Price on the day usually decides it. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack T Series review</a>
            and <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack vs ProForm</a>
            comparison cover the brand question.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Against the NordicTrack Commercial series</h3>
          <p>
            If decline, a 22-inch belt or a longer motor warranty matters, NordicTrack's
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1250</a>
            adds decline, a wider belt and a 400 lb rating, and the
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1750</a>
            adds lifetime motor cover, both at higher prices.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Without a subscription</h3>
          <p>
            If you know you will never subscribe to iFIT, the
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6</a>
            works fully without one and covers its motor for life. Our guide to
            <a href="/is-proform-a-good-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether ProForm is a good brand</a>
            covers ProForm's strengths and weaknesses more broadly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Buying Tips, Sales and Buying Used</h2>
<p>
            ProForm runs promotions most of the year, and its list prices are rarely what anyone pays. On the days we
            checked, ProForm's own site showed about $1,600 and Nebraska Furniture Mart $1,799. Sale events such as
            Black Friday tend to bring further cuts or bundles, but we cannot say what the 9000 will cost on a future
            date. Judge a deal against those prices and against the Carbon Pro 2000 on the same day. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill guide</a>
            explains how to tell a real discount from a reframed list price.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buying a used Pro 9000? Find out which one it is first, using the screen size, the incline range and the
            model number. An older 22-inch Pro 9000 is not a worse machine for a walker, and its decline is a feature
            the current one lacks, but its 300 lb rating is lower and any warranty has probably expired or will not
            transfer. Ask ProForm with the serial number, assume no cover until told otherwise, and inspect the belt,
            deck, incline and screen. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            runs through it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Once you own one, our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance guide</a>
            covers lubrication and belt adjustment, and our
            <a href="/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm troubleshooting guide</a>
            covers resets and the common faults. Before servicing anything, unplug the machine and remove the safety
            key; if you notice a burning smell, sparking or a tripping breaker, stop using it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Not</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the Carbon Pro 9000 if you walk, incline-walk or run moderately, want iFIT on a big screen,
            and want a 350 lb rating on a folding full-size deck.</strong> It is ProForm's most complete current
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose the Carbon Pro 2000 instead if you will not use the screen much.</strong> On ProForm's
            figures it has the same deck, incline, speed and rating for less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if you want decline, a 22-inch belt or long motor cover.</strong> The current 9000
            has none of them; NordicTrack's Commercial series has all three at higher prices.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if you will never subscribe.</strong> Much of what you pay for sits idle without
            a membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever you decide, check which Pro 9000 a listing or review describes.</strong> The older
            22-inch machine and the current Carbon Pro 9000 share a name and not much else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the ProForm Pro 9000 and the Carbon Pro 9000?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The older Pro 9000 had a 22-inch touchscreen, incline from -3% to 12% and a 300 lb rating. The current Carbon Pro 9000 (PFTL16925) has a 16-inch touchscreen, incline from 0 to 12% with no decline and a 350 lb rating. Both have a 20 by 60-inch belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the ProForm Carbon Pro 9000 treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walkers and moderate runners who will use iFIT on a big screen, it is a well-specified folding treadmill with a 350 lb rating. It is worth less if you will not keep a membership, and its motor warranty is two years. If you will not use the screen much, the Carbon Pro 2000 is better value. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the ProForm Carbon Pro 9000?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ProForm’s product sheet for PFTL16925 and Nebraska Furniture Mart’s listing both give 350 lb. The older Pro 9000 was rated to 300 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
